::MCR_U03_L02_GP_Q01::
Question ID: MCR_U03_L02_GP_Q01
MLA Standard: MLA.MCR.DL.4
Question:
Find the mean of 4, 6, 8, and 10.
{
=A. 7#Correct. 4 + 6 + 8 + 10 = 28, and 28 / 4 = 7.
~B. 6#This is below the balance point. Add all values and divide by 4.
~C. 8#This is one data value, not the mean of all values.
~D. 28#This is the sum. Divide the sum by the number of values.
}
::MCR_U03_L02_GP_Q02::
Question ID: MCR_U03_L02_GP_Q02
MLA Standard: MLA.MCR.DL.4
Question:
Find the median of 3, 9, 5, 12, 7.
{
~A. 5#The data must be ordered before finding the middle.
=B. 7#Correct. Ordered data are 3, 5, 7, 9, 12, so the middle value is 7.
~C. 9#This is above the middle after ordering.
~D. 12#This is the greatest value, not the median.
}
::MCR_U03_L02_GP_Q03::
Question ID: MCR_U03_L02_GP_Q03
MLA Standard: MLA.MCR.DL.4
Question:
Find the range of 11, 14, 18, 20.
{
~A. 4#This counts the values, not the spread.
~B. 31#This adds the smallest and greatest instead of subtracting.
=C. 9#Correct. Range is greatest minus least: 20 - 11 = 9.
~D. 15.75#This is the mean, not the range.
}
::MCR_U03_L02_GP_Q04::
Question ID: MCR_U03_L02_GP_Q04
MLA Standard: MLA.MCR.DL.4
Question:
Which measure is often better than the mean when a data set has an extreme outlier?
{
~A. range#Range describes spread, not center.
~B. mode#Mode may not describe the center well, especially if values do not repeat.
~C. maximum#The maximum is the greatest value, not a measure of center.
=D. median#Correct. The median is resistant to extreme values.
}
::MCR_U03_L02_GP_Q05::
Question ID: MCR_U03_L02_GP_Q05
MLA Standard: MLA.MCR.DL.4
Question:
Two data sets have the same mean, but one has a much larger range. What does the larger range show?
{
~A. higher center#The center can be the same even when spread is different.
=B. more variability#Correct. A larger range means the values are more spread out.
~C. no outliers possible#A larger range does not rule out outliers.
~D. fewer data values#Range does not tell how many values are in the data set.
}
